<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Biscay</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bizkaia, Vizcaya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Biscay (Basque: Bizkaia) is a province of the Basque Country in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilbao">Bilbao</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was a lordship ruled by the Haro family; it passed to the Crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> in 1379 but kept its own fueros, or customary laws, and its assembly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guernica">Guernica</a> until the 19th century. Its iron mines and ports made it the industrial heart of the Basque Country.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/biscay.jpg" alt="Biscay" width="320" height="275" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Biscay_in_Spain_(plus_Canarias).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
